🔹 What is @Embedded in JPA?

@Embedded is used when you want to reuse a group of columns (value object) inside an entity, without making it a separate table.
It lets you embed another class’s fields into your entity’s table.

Example:

Embeddable Class

@Embeddable
public class Address {
    private String street;
    private String city;
    private String zipCode;

    // getters & setters
}


👉 This is not an entity, but a reusable component.

Entity with @Embedded

@Entity
public class Person {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @Embedded   // fields of Address will be stored in Person table
    private Address address;

    // getters & setters
}

Table Created (person)
| id | name  | street      | city     | zipCode |
| -- | ----- | ----------- | -------- | ------- |
| 1  | John  | Main Street | New York | 12345   |
| 2  | Sarah | Park Ave    | Chicago  | 67890   |


👉 Notice: Address did not create a separate table.
Its fields (street, city, zipCode) are stored in the same table (person).

🔹 Benefits of @Embedded

✅ Code reuse – You can use Address in multiple entities (Person, Company, Employee).
✅ Keeps related columns grouped logically.
✅ Still only one table – no joins needed.

🔹 Related Annotation

@Embeddable → marks a class as embeddable.

@Embedded → used inside an entity to include that class.

@AttributeOverrides → lets you rename embedded column names if you embed multiple times.

👉 Example with override:

@Embedded
@AttributeOverrides({
    @AttributeOverride(name = "street", column = @Column(name = "home_street")),
    @AttributeOverride(name = "city", column = @Column(name = "home_city"))
})
private Address homeAddress;


⚡ In short:
@Embedded = Put another class’s fields inside the same table (not another table).